openvino_bevfusion: make the sparse encoder's kernel choice reproducible.

The sparse encoder picks one of five SubM convolution kernels per channel
width by timing them on the first frame. They come in two kinds: w8 and w4
accumulate in FP32 throughout, while lite, lite8 and fast add FP16 sums over
each neighbour's input channels in FP32, as the fused conv2+residual kernel
always does. The two kinds give different results, so a pick that depends
on timing made the same input give different detections in different
processes. On a 10-sweep nuScenes frame both kinds are equally close to
spconv (relL2 2.85e-3 and 2.98e-3, mostly the FP16 feature storage).

Let only lite, lite8 and fast compete. Within that kind the results are
bit-identical, so the timing only decides the speed. Also time the kernels
again when a frame has at least twice the voxels of the one they were timed
on: the first frame of a sequence is often much smaller than the rest
(12.7k voxels against about 80k with ten sweeps), and the fastest kernel
depends on the size.

This removes one source of run-to-run differences. Voxelization is another:
it averages the first 10 points of a voxel in whatever order the GPU
inserts them, which changes about 100 of 100k voxels between runs.

Remove this patch once openvino_contrib carries an equivalent change.

